\title{The Register Threshold of Order Finding:\\
Truncation, Sample Fungibility, and an Exact Success Density}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{13 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Shor's order-finding algorithm is conventionally run with a phase register of
$\ell = 2\lceil \log_2 N\rceil$ qubits. We ask whether that register can be
truncated: if only the top $t$ bits of the Fourier outcome are measured, can the
order $r$ still be recovered by taking more samples? We answer the question
completely, with matching upper and lower bounds, in two independent measurement
models.

We prove a two-sided \emph{register threshold}: for any bound $R$ on the order, a
$t$-bit register determines the continued-fraction target whenever $R^2 \le 2^t$
(equivalently $t \ge 2\log_2 R + 2$), and fails to determine it whenever
$2^t < R(R-1)$ (equivalently $t+1 \le 2\log_2 R$). Hence
$t_{\min} = 2\log_2 R + O(1)$; the prediction $t_{\min} = \log_2 r + O(\log\log r)$
is refuted, since a register of $\log_2 R + c$ bits is ambiguous for every
constant $c$ as soon as $R > 2^c+1$. The same quadratic threshold holds in the
strictest dyadic-grid model, via an elementary Farey count showing that at least
$R^2/4$ distinct reduced fractions of denominator $\le 2R$ lie in $(0,1)$.

Below the weaker collapse threshold $2^t \le r$ the situation is worse than
ambiguity: the truncated outcome map is surjective onto the whole $t$-bit
alphabet, so the sets of observable records coincide for all orders in
$[2^t,R]$, and no estimator with any sample budget and unbounded computation is
correct for two distinct such orders. A truncated sample carries exactly
$\min(2^t,r)$ symbols, so identifying an order out of a family $S$ costs
$m\,t \ge \log_2|S|$ bits in total; and a permanent divisor ambiguity shows that
no support-only estimator ever exists, at any register size.

Above the threshold the only remaining obstruction is $\gcd(k,r)>1$, and
sampling repairs it: a record recovers the order if and only if the sampled
numerators are jointly coprime to $r$, equivalently if and only if the sampled
residues generate $\Z/r\Z$. We count the successful records exactly: their
number is Jordan's totient
$J_m(r)=\sum_{d\mid r}\mu(d)(r/d)^m = r^m\prod_{p\mid r}(1-p^{-m})$, so the
failure probability of $m$ samples is exactly $1-\prod_{p\mid r}(1-p^{-m})$,
which is below $2^{-(m-1)}$ uniformly in $r$. The final ledger is
$2\log_2 r + O(1)$ qubits (rigid) against $O(1)$ samples (nearly free). Since
$r\sim N$ for a random base modulo a semiprime, $t_{\min}\approx 2\log_2 N$:
Shor's register is forced.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The question}

Shor's factoring algorithm reduces factoring an integer $N$ to \emph{order
finding}: given $a$ with $\gcd(a,N)=1$, find the least $r\ge 1$ with
$a^r\equiv 1\pmod N$. The quantum subroutine is phase estimation applied to
multiplication by $a$; it returns a measurement outcome encoding a real number
\[
  x \;\approx\; \frac{k}{r},\qquad 0\le k<r,
\]
with $k$ approximately uniform, and the classical post-processing recovers $k/r$
--- hence $r$ --- by continued fractions. The standard analysis takes the phase
register to have $\ell = 2\lceil\log_2 N\rceil$ qubits, guaranteeing the accuracy
$|x-k/r|<1/(2r^2)$ under which the continued-fraction step is provably correct.

Qubits are the scarcest resource in every projected implementation, so the
natural engineering question is whether $\ell$ can be reduced by \emph{truncating}
the register: measuring only the top $t$ bits and compensating with more samples.
Since $m$ samples of $t$ bits carry $mt$ bits in total, one might hope for a
smooth qubit--sample trade curve, say $t=\log_2 r+O(\log\log r)$ with
polynomially many samples.

\subsection{The answer}

There is no such trade curve. The behaviour is a sharp trichotomy with a wall at
$t\approx 2\log_2 r$:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{$t\le\log_2 r$ (collapse).} The observable data are
  \emph{identical} for all orders $\ge 2^t$; no estimator, at any sample budget,
  is correct for two of them.
  \item \textbf{$\log_2 r<t<2\log_2 r$ (single-shot ambiguity).} The
  continued-fraction target is undetermined by a $t$-bit reading.
  \item \textbf{$t\ge 2\log_2 r+O(1)$ (recovery).} The target is determined, and
  the residual gcd defect is repaired by a constant number of samples, with
  failure probability below $2^{-(m-1)}$.
\end{enumerate}
The threshold's location is not quantum-mechanical in origin: distinct rationals
of denominator $\le R$ are separated by at least $1/R^2$, so an interval of width
$2^{-t}$ contains at most one of them precisely when $2^t\gtrsim R^2$. The
exponent $2$ is the product of the two denominators in that separation bound.

\section{The models}

Throughout, $r\ge1$ is the true order, $k$ a sampled numerator with $0\le k<r$,
and $R$ an a-priori bound on the order (in the factoring application $R=N$).

\begin{definition}[Order fraction]
For $k,r\in\N$ with $r>0$, the \emph{order fraction} is $k/r\in\Q$. Its reduced
denominator is $\den(k/r)=r/\gcd(k,r)$; it equals $r$ exactly when
$\gcd(k,r)=1$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Tolerance model]
A $t$-bit phase register has resolution $\res(t)=2^{-(t+1)}$. A rational $q$ is
\emph{compatible} with an observed phase $x\in\R$ if $|x-q|<\res(t)$. Two
rationals are \emph{confusable at $t$ bits} if some $x$ is compatible with both.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Grid model]
A $t$-bit \emph{truncated register} reports, for the exact phase $k/r$, the cell
index
\[
  \out_t(r,k)=\Bigl\lfloor \frac{2^t k}{r}\Bigr\rfloor\in\{0,\dots,2^t-1\}
  \qquad(0\le k<r),
\]
and two phases are confusable exactly when $\lfloor 2^tx\rfloor=\lfloor
2^tx'\rfloor$. Write $\mathcal{O}_t(r)=\{\out_t(r,k):0\le k<r\}$. A \emph{record}
is a finite list of outcomes; an \emph{estimator} is an arbitrary function from
records to $\N$, with no computational restriction.
\end{definition}

The grid model is the harsher of the two; Section~\ref{sec:grid} shows the
threshold is the same in both, and Lemma~\ref{lem:models-agree} shows the two
models describe the same measurement on order fractions.

\section{The resolution threshold}

\subsection{Separation of rationals}

\begin{theorem}[Farey separation]\label{thm:farey}
For distinct rationals $a\ne b$,
$\;|a-b|\ \ge\ \bigl(\den(a)\den(b)\bigr)^{-1}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Write $a=p/q$, $b=p'/q'$ in lowest terms. Then $(a-b)qq'=pq'-p'q=:z\in\Z$ and
$z\ne0$, so $|a-b|\,qq'=|z|\ge1$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Sufficiency}

\begin{theorem}[Unique continued-fraction target]\label{thm:unique}
Let $R^2\le 2^t$. If $q_1,q_2$ are rationals of reduced denominator at most $R$,
both compatible with the same phase $x$ at $t$ bits, then $q_1=q_2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $q_1\ne q_2$, the triangle inequality gives
$|q_1-q_2|\le|x-q_1|+|x-q_2|<2\cdot2^{-(t+1)}=2^{-t}$, while
Theorem~\ref{thm:farey} gives $|q_1-q_2|\ge 1/(\den q_1\den q_2)\ge R^{-2}\ge
2^{-t}$, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Order-level uniqueness]
Let $R^2\le 2^t$. If two samples $k_1/r_1$, $k_2/r_2$ have numerators coprime to
their orders, orders bounded by $R$, and are both compatible with the same phase,
then $r_1=r_2$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Sufficiency in bits]\label{cor:suff-bits}
If $t\ge 2\lfloor\log_2 R\rfloor+2$, a $t$-bit register determines the
continued-fraction target among all fractions of reduced denominator $\le R$.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{Necessity}

\begin{lemma}[Nearest pair]
For $R\ge2$, $\;\frac{1}{R-1}-\frac{1}{R}=\frac{1}{R(R-1)}>0$. Both fractions are
reduced, of denominators $R$ and $R-1$, and both are honest order fractions
(numerator $k=1$ at orders $R$ and $R-1$).
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Ambiguity below the threshold]\label{thm:ambig}
Let $R\ge2$ and $2^t<R(R-1)$. Then some phase $x$ is compatible at $t$ bits with
both $1/R$ and $1/(R-1)$: a $t$-bit register cannot decide between the orders $R$
and $R-1$, and no post-processing can.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Take $x$ to be the midpoint. Each fraction is at distance $(2R(R-1))^{-1}$ from
$x$, and $2^t<R(R-1)$ gives $(2R(R-1))^{-1}<2^{-(t+1)}=\res(t)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Two-sided threshold]\label{thm:two-sided}
For every $R\ge3$: (a) if $t\ge2\lfloor\log_2R\rfloor+2$, all fractions of
reduced denominator $\le R$ compatible with one phase coincide; (b) if
$t+1\le2\lfloor\log_2R\rfloor$, some phase is compatible with two distinct such
fractions. Hence
$\;2\log_2R-1\le t_{\min}\le 2\log_2R+2$, i.e.\ $t_{\min}=2\log_2R+O(1)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
(a) is Corollary~\ref{cor:suff-bits}. For (b), $2^{\lfloor\log_2R\rfloor}\le R$
gives $2^{t+1}\le 2^{2\lfloor\log_2R\rfloor}\le R^2$, so $2^t\le R^2/2<R(R-1)$
for $R\ge3$; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:ambig}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[No linear register]
If $R>2^c+1$ and $2^t\le 2^cR$, some phase is compatible with two distinct
fractions of reduced denominator $\le R$. Thus a register of $\log_2R+c$ bits is
ambiguous for every constant $c$, and no additive correction of size $o(\log R)$
--- in particular none of size $O(\log\log R)$ --- suffices.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$2^c<R-1$ gives $2^t\le 2^cR<R(R-1)$; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:ambig}.
\end{proof}

\section{Below the threshold: collapse, capacity, permanent ambiguity}

\subsection{Surjectivity and collapse}

\begin{theorem}[Surjectivity]\label{thm:surj}
If $2^t\le r$, then for every $m<2^t$ there is $k<r$ with $\out_t(r,k)=m$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The window $[mr/2^t,(m+1)r/2^t)$ has length $r/2^t\ge1$, hence contains an
integer $k$, which satisfies $m\le 2^tk/r<m+1$; and $k<r$ because $m<2^t$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Order-independent alphabet]\label{cor:alphabet}
If $2^t\le r$ then $\mathcal{O}_t(r)=\{0,\dots,2^t-1\}$, independent of $r$.
Consequently, for $2^t\le r$ and $2^t\le r'$, a list of outcomes is realisable at
order $r$ if and only if it is realisable at order $r'$, for records of arbitrary
length.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Samples do not help]\label{thm:nohelp}
Let $r\ne r'$ with $2^t\le r$ and $2^t\le r'$. There is no estimator $A$ (an
arbitrary function from records to $\N$, with no bound on record length or
computation) returning $r$ on every record realisable at order $r$ and $r'$ on
every record realisable at order $r'$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Any record realisable at $r$ is realisable at $r'$ by
Corollary~\ref{cor:alphabet}, so $r=A(L)=r'$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Collapse window]
All $R-2^t+1$ orders in $[2^t,R]$ emit exactly the same set of records, namely
all lists over $\{0,\dots,2^t-1\}$.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{Capacity of a truncated sample}

\begin{theorem}[Exact capacity]\label{thm:capacity}
For $r>0$, $\;\bigl|\{\out_t(r,k):0\le k<r\}\bigr|=\min(2^t,r)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $2^t\le r$, the image is the full alphabet by Corollary~\ref{cor:alphabet}. If
$r\le 2^t$, then $2^t(k+1)\ge 2^tk+r$ forces
$\lfloor2^t(k+1)/r\rfloor>\lfloor2^tk/r\rfloor$, so the map is strictly
increasing on $[0,r)$ and its image has $r$ elements.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The product bound}

\begin{theorem}[Sample-count lower bound]
Let $S$ be a finite family of candidate orders, $A$ an arbitrary estimator, and
suppose for each $r\in S$ there is a record $L(r)$ of exactly $m$ symbols from the
$t$-bit alphabet with $A(L(r))=r$. Then $|S|\le (2^t)^m$, i.e.\
$m\,t\ge\log_2|S|$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$r\mapsto L(r)$ is injective and lands in a set of size $(2^t)^m$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
Identifying every order in $[2^t,R]$ from $m$ truncated samples requires
$R-2^t+1\le (2^t)^m$. Inside the collapse window this can never be achieved,
since by Theorem~\ref{thm:nohelp} no such estimator exists at any $m$.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{Divisor ambiguity}

\begin{theorem}[Nested supports]\label{thm:nested}
If $r\mid r'$ and $r'>0$, then $\mathcal{O}_t(r)\subseteq\mathcal{O}_t(r')$ for
\emph{every} $t$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Writing $r'=sr$, we have $k/r=(sk)/(sr)$ exactly, and $sk<r'$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[No support-only estimator]
For $r>0$ and $s>1$, no estimator depending only on the \emph{set} of observed
outcomes (rather than their multiplicities) is correct for both $r$ and $rs$, at
any register size and any sample budget.
\end{corollary}

\section{Above the threshold: what samples buy}

\begin{definition}
The \emph{recovered order} from one sample is $\rho(k,r):=\den(k/r)=r/\gcd(k,r)$.
For a record $K=(k_1,\dots,k_m)$ the \emph{record estimate} is
$\widehat r(K):=\lcmop\bigl(\rho(k_1,r),\dots,\rho(k_m,r)\bigr)$, with
$\widehat r(\varnothing)=1$, and the \emph{record gcd} is
$g(K):=\gcd(k_1,\dots,k_m)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}
$\rho(k,r)\mid r$ always; $\rho(k,r)=r$ iff $\gcd(k,r)=1$; and $\rho(k,r)<r$
strictly whenever $\gcd(k,r)>1$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[Sample criterion, sharp]\label{thm:criterion}
For $r>0$ and any record $K$,
$\;\widehat r(K)=r \iff \gcd\bigl(g(K),r\bigr)=1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
($\Leftarrow$) Each $\rho(k_i,r)\mid r$, so $d:=\widehat r(K)\mid r$. If $d<r$,
pick a prime $p\mid r/d$; from $\rho(k_i,r)\mid d\mid r$ we get
$(r/d)\mid r/\rho(k_i,r)=\gcd(k_i,r)$, hence $p\mid k_i$ for all $i$, so
$p\mid\gcd(g(K),r)=1$, absurd.
($\Rightarrow$) If a prime $p$ divides $\gcd(g(K),r)$, then $\rho(k_i,r)\mid r/p$
for every $i$, so $\widehat r(K)\mid r/p<r$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Two samples]
If $\gcd(\gcd(k_1,k_2),r)=1$ then $\lcmop(\rho(k_1,r),\rho(k_2,r))=r$, even if
each sample alone under-reports.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Generation criterion]\label{thm:generate}
For $r>0$, $\rho(k,r)$ is the additive order of $k$ in $\Z/r\Z$; the residues of
a record $K$ generate $\Z/r\Z$ if and only if $\gcd(g(K),r)=1$; and consequently
\[
  \widehat r(K)=r \iff \langle \bar k_1,\dots,\bar k_m\rangle = \Z/r\Z .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The additive order of $k$ modulo $r$ is $r/\gcd(k,r)$. By B\'ezout along the
record, $\overline{g(K)}$ lies in the subgroup generated by the residues; if
$\gcd(g(K),r)=1$ some multiple of $g(K)$ is $\equiv1$, so the subgroup is
everything. Conversely if the subgroup is everything, $1$ is an integer
combination of the $k_i$ modulo $r$, forcing $\gcd(g(K),r)=1$. Combine with
Theorem~\ref{thm:criterion}.
\end{proof}

\section{How many samples? An exact count}

Fix $r\ge1$, $m\ge1$, let $\mathcal{A}(r,m)=\{0,\dots,r-1\}^m$ and
$\mathcal{G}(r,m)=\{K\in\mathcal{A}(r,m):\gcd(g(K),r)=1\}$ (the \emph{good}
records, i.e.\ exactly those that recover the order), and
$\mathcal{B}=\mathcal{A}\setminus\mathcal{G}$. Write $\omega(r)$ for the number
of distinct prime divisors of $r$.

\begin{theorem}[Union bound]
For $r>0$, $\;|\mathcal{B}(r,m)|\cdot 2^m\le\omega(r)\,r^m$. Hence a good record
of length $m$ exists whenever $\omega(r)<2^m$, and since
$\omega(r)\le\lfloor\log_2 r\rfloor$, $m=\lfloor\log_2\log_2 r\rfloor+1$ samples
always suffice.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$K$ is bad iff some prime $p\mid r$ divides every entry; for fixed $p$ there are
$(r/p)^m\le (r/2)^m$ such records. Sum over the $\omega(r)$ primes.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Prime tail]\label{lem:tail}
For every finite set $S$ of primes, $\sum_{p\in S}p^{-2}<\tfrac12$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
The primes $2,3,5,7$ contribute $\tfrac14+\tfrac19+\tfrac1{25}+\tfrac1{49}
=\tfrac{18589}{44100}<0.4216$. Every other prime is an odd number $\ge11$, i.e.\
$2k+1$ with $k\ge5$, distinct primes giving distinct $k$; from
$(2k+1)^{-2}\le (4k)^{-1}-(4(k+1))^{-1}$ the remaining terms telescope to at most
$1/20$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Majority success]\label{thm:majority}
For every $r\ge1$ and every $m\ge2$, $\;r^m<2\,|\mathcal{G}(r,m)|$: strictly more
than half of all length-$m$ records recover the order, uniformly in $r$ and with
no dependence on $\omega(r)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$|\mathcal{B}|\le\sum_{p\mid r}(r/p)^m\le r^m\sum_{p\mid r}p^{-2}<r^m/2$ by
Lemma~\ref{lem:tail}; use $|\mathcal{G}|+|\mathcal{B}|=r^m$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exponential concentration]
For $r\ge1$ and $m\ge2$, $\;2^{m-1}|\mathcal{B}(r,m)|<r^m$; the failure
probability of $m$ samples is below $2^{-(m-1)}$, uniformly in $r$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$\sum_{p\mid r}p^{-m}\le 2^{-(m-2)}\sum_{p\mid r}p^{-2}<2^{-(m-1)}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Divisor identity]\label{thm:divid}
For $n>0$ and any $m$, $\;\sum_{d\mid n}|\mathcal{G}(d,m)|=n^m$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Partition $\mathcal{A}(n,m)$ by $e=\gcd(g(K),n)$. Dividing every entry by $e$ is
a bijection from the level set onto $\mathcal{G}(n/e,m)$, since dividing all
entries by $e$ divides the record gcd by $e$ and multiplying by $e$ multiplies it
back. Sum over divisors and reindex $e\mapsto n/e$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[M\"obius form and Euler product]\label{thm:jordan}
For $r>0$,
\[
  |\mathcal{G}(r,m)| \;=\; \sum_{d\mid r}\mu(d)\Bigl(\frac{r}{d}\Bigr)^{m}
  \;=\; r^m\prod_{p\mid r}\Bigl(1-\frac{1}{p^{m}}\Bigr) \;=\; J_m(r),
\]
Jordan's totient. Equivalently, the success probability of a uniformly random
length-$m$ record is exactly $\prod_{p\mid r}(1-p^{-m})$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
M\"obius inversion of Theorem~\ref{thm:divid} gives the first equality. In that
sum only squarefree divisors contribute; these are the products of subsets $T$ of
the prime divisors of $r$, with $\mu(\prod_{p\in T}p)=(-1)^{|T|}$, so the sum
factors as the stated Euler product.
\end{proof}

For $m=1$ this is Euler's $\varphi$, reproducing the classical statement that a
single Shor sample succeeds with probability $\varphi(r)/r$.

\section{The same threshold in the strictest model}\label{sec:grid}

Let $C(R)=\#\{(a,b)\in[1,R]^2:\gcd(a,b)=1\}$.

\begin{lemma}[gcd fibration]\label{lem:fib}
$R^2=\sum_{d=1}^{R} C\bigl(\lfloor R/d\rfloor\bigr)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Every pair in $[1,R]^2$ has a unique gcd $d$; dividing by $d$ is a bijection onto
the coprime pairs in $[1,\lfloor R/d\rfloor]^2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[A quarter of pairs are coprime]\label{thm:quarter}
$R^2\le 4\,C(R)$ for every $R$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:fib} and $\sum_{d\ge2}d^{-2}\le\tfrac34$,
$R^2\le C(R)+\sum_{d\ge2}\lfloor R/d\rfloor^2\le C(R)+\tfrac34R^2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:many}
The map $(a,b)\mapsto a/(a+b)$ sends distinct coprime pairs in $[1,R]^2$ to
distinct reduced fractions in $(0,1)$ of denominator at most $2R$; hence at least
$R^2/4$ such fractions exist.
\end{corollary}

\begin{lemma}[The models agree]\label{lem:models-agree}
For all $t,r,k$: $\;\lfloor 2^t\cdot (k/r)\rfloor=\out_t(r,k)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Two-sided grid threshold]
If $R^2\le 2^t$, distinct reduced fractions of denominator $\le R$ always lie in
distinct dyadic cells. If $4\cdot2^t<R^2$, two distinct reduced fractions of
denominator $\le 2R$ share a cell. Consequently, if a $t$-bit grid register
separates all reduced fractions of denominator $\le D$ in $(0,1)$, then
$2\log_2 D<t+6$; and if $4\cdot 2^t<R^2$ there are distinct order fractions
$k/r\ne k'/r'$ with $r,r'\le 2R$ producing the same register outcome. The
critical number of cells lies between $R^2/4$ and $R^2$, so again
$t_{\min}=2\log_2R+O(1)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Separation: two rationals in one cell differ by less than $2^{-t}\le R^{-2}$,
contradicting Theorem~\ref{thm:farey}. Ambiguity: by
Corollary~\ref{cor:many} there are more than $2^t$ fractions competing for $2^t$
cells; pigeonhole. The order-fraction statement follows by
Lemma~\ref{lem:models-agree}.
\end{proof}

\section{Both axes of the channel are forced}

The threshold is a \emph{depth} bound: each retained frequency must be read to
$2\log_2 r$ bits. It composes with the \emph{width} bound for Fourier sampling:
a scheme whose sampled frequencies determine an arbitrary period-$r$ signal on
$\Z/r\Z$ must query at least $r$ frequencies.

\begin{theorem}[Irreducible channel]
Consider a Fourier-sampling scheme on $\Z/r\Z$ with $K$ frequency indices whose
readings determine the signal. Then $K\ge r$. Moreover if each outcome is read to
only $t$ bits with $t+1\le 2\lfloor\log_2R\rfloor$, where $R\ge3$ bounds the
order, the continued-fraction target is undetermined. Neither axis can be traded
for the other.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Realised at genuine instances]
For every $r\ge3$, the Mersenne modulus $N=2^r-1$ with base $a=2$ has
multiplicative order exactly $r$. Hence at every scale there is an honest
order-finding instance to which the depth bound applies.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Trichotomy]
Fix $R\ge3$. (i) For all $t$ and distinct $r,r'\ge2^t$, no estimator using any
number of truncated samples is correct for both. (ii) For $t+1\le
2\lfloor\log_2R\rfloor$, some phase is compatible with two distinct fractions of
denominator $\le R$. (iii) For $t\ge 2\lfloor\log_2R\rfloor+2$ and $0<r\le R$: if
the post-processor returns, for each sample $k$ of a record, some fraction of
denominator $\le R$ compatible with the same observed phase as $k/r$, and the
numerators are jointly coprime to $r$, then the least common multiple of the
returned denominators is exactly $r$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch of (iii)]
Uniqueness (Theorem~\ref{thm:unique}) forces the returned fraction to be the true
reduced $k/r$, whose denominator is $\rho(k,r)$;
Theorem~\ref{thm:criterion} then assembles $r$ from the lcm.
\end{proof}

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{Consequence for Shor's algorithm}

For a random base $a$ modulo a semiprime $N$, the order $r$ is typically
comparable to $N$. Substituting $r\sim N$ into Theorem~\ref{thm:two-sided},
\[
  t_{\min}\;\approx\;2\log_2 N\;\approx\;\ell = 2\lceil\log_2N\rceil .
\]
The conventional register size is not a comfortable margin; it is the minimum.
Moreover the failure mode below the threshold is not degraded performance: by
Theorem~\ref{thm:nohelp} the observable records of different orders are
identical, so the estimation problem has no solution at all, at any sample budget
and any computational power.

\subsection{The ledger}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\toprule
resource & requirement & character\\
\midrule
register depth & $2\log_2 r+O(1)$ bits & rigid \\
channel width & $\ge r$ frequencies & rigid \\
samples & $m\ge2$ for success $>1/2$; failure $<2^{-(m-1)}$ & nearly free \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Fungibility holds only in the last row, and only above the threshold, where by
Theorem~\ref{thm:generate} it means precisely that samples enlarge the observed
subgroup of $\Z/r\Z$.

\subsection{Scope and limitations}

The lower bounds are information-theoretic and unconditional: they hold for
arbitrary, even non-uniform and computationally unbounded post-processing, and in
both the mildest and the harshest models of truncation. They do not rule out a
different quantum subroutine that produces no phase estimate at all. The density
statements of Section~6 assume numerators drawn uniformly from
$\{0,\dots,r-1\}$, matching the idealised phase-estimation distribution; the
support-level arguments of Section~4 are insensitive to the distribution.

\subsection{Future directions}

Extending the analysis to noisy phase distributions and to partially truncated
registers where low-order bits are measured with error rather than discarded;
quantifying the interaction between the depth threshold and error-correction
overheads; sharpening the grid-model constants from the interval $[R^2/4,R^2]$ to
the exact critical cell count; and exploring whether the divisor ambiguity has an
analogue for hidden-subgroup problems beyond the cyclic case.

\section{Conclusion}

Truncating the phase register does not trade qubits for samples; it destroys the
algorithm. The wall sits at $t_{\min}=2\log_2r+O(1)$, dictated by the separation
of rationals of bounded denominator, and it holds in both the tolerance and the
dyadic-grid models and for genuine arithmetic instances of every order. Below the
wall, the observable data of distinct orders coincide identically. Above it, the
residual gcd obstruction is characterised exactly --- a record succeeds iff its
residues generate $\Z/r\Z$ --- and counted exactly by Jordan's totient
$J_m(r)=r^m\prod_{p\mid r}(1-p^{-m})$, giving failure probability below
$2^{-(m-1)}$ uniformly in the order. Since $r$ is typically comparable to $N$,
the required register is $\approx 2\log_2N$ bits: exactly the register Shor
specified, and not one bit fewer.

\end{document}

